**What**

The required and optional fields on `issue new` are inverted relative to what
an issue tracker needs, and `review new --target` accepts literally anything by
falling through to a bare commit variant without ever consulting the
repository.

**Where**

- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:245` -- `args.title.unwrap_or_default()`, so a
  missing title becomes an empty string
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:246` -- `--body is required unless running interactively`
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:524` -- interactive `Title` uses `prompt_optional_text`
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:438` -- interactive body rejects empty input
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:1043` -- `parse_review_target` unrecognised-input fallback

**Reproduction**

    git-forge issue new --body "no title at all" --reporter Tester
    28f76833                                     # accepted

    git-forge issue new --title "only title"
    Error: --body is required unless running interactively

    git-forge review new --body b --target not-an-oid-at-all
    6515c2c5                                     # accepted

    git-forge review list
    | #6515c2c5 | commit:not-an-oid-at-all | alice | open |

An untitled issue then renders as `(untitled)` in `list` and `show`, which is
handled gracefully -- but it should not have been creatable. A review can point
at an object that is not an oid, let alone one that exists in the repository.

Note also that `--target commit:HEAD` is accepted and stored as the literal
string `HEAD`, never resolved. There is no `rev_parse` anywhere on this path,
so short oids and revision expressions -- the way people actually refer to
commits -- silently store garbage.

**Why it matters**

The title is what appears in `list`, in `query`, and in any cross-reference. It
is the identity of the issue to a human. Making it optional while making the
body mandatory means the cheapest thing to file is an issue that is unreadable
in the list view, and the highest-friction thing is the quick one-line note
people actually want to jot. Compare `gh issue create`, which requires a title
and lets the body be empty. That inversion will be felt within the first minute.

The review target is worse because it is silent corruption rather than
friction. A review that targets `not-an-oid-at-all` looks entirely normal in
`review list`. Since reviews exist to point at code, a review pointing at
nothing is the failure the type was designed to prevent, and `ReviewTarget`
already models six precise variants -- the parser just gives up and guesses on
anything it does not recognise.

**Options**

- Option A -- Require `--title`, make `--body` optional and default to empty,
  and require the interactive title prompt. Trade-off: a breaking change to a
  tool with no users yet, so effectively free.
- Option B -- Resolve every target through `repo.rev_parse_single` and reject
  what does not resolve, so `commit:HEAD` and short oids work and typos fail
  loudly. Trade-off: reviews can no longer be filed against objects not yet
  fetched, which is occasionally legitimate -- gate that behind `--force`.
- Option C -- Make the unrecognised-prefix fallback an error rather than a
  guess, so `--target <bare-string>` must be explicitly `commit:<oid>`.
  Trade-off: slightly more typing, much less ambiguity.

**Recommendation**

All three. High confidence -- none of these are close calls, and B in
particular converts a silent-corruption path into a helpful one while also
making `--target HEAD~1` work, which is what anyone would try first.